{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "190",
    "case_summary": "A 71-year-old woman with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and ischemic heart disease presented with a 2-month history of intermittent painless hematuria with passage of clots. She denied dysuria, frequency, or other lower urinary tract symptoms, and there was no family history of malignancy. On examination, vital signs and abdominal and pelvic exams were unremarkable. Laboratory evaluation showed a hemoglobin level of 8.6 g/dL requiring blood transfusion; serum creatinine and other biochemical parameters were within normal limits. Transabdominal ultrasonography revealed a 2.9 × 2.5 × 2.4 cm polypoidal mass arising from the posterolateral wall of the bladder dome; both kidneys and the upper tracts appeared normal. Pre- and postvoid bladder volumes were 180 mL and 44 mL, respectively. Cystoscopy demonstrated an exophytic solid tumor measuring 1.5 × 2.0 cm on the anterior fundal wall, covered with slough. A deep transurethral resection of the bladder tumor was performed. Histopathological analysis revealed an adenocarcinoma composed of mucinous and signet-ring cell components. Thus, a secondary adenocarcinoma or a rare variant of primary adenocarcinoma of the bladder was suspected.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Primary signet-ring cell carcinoma of the bladder\nB. secondary bladder involvement by adjacent or distant malignancies\nC. Need to exclude a metastasis before diagnosing a primary bladder tumor\nD. Potential primary signet\nE. Utility and limitations of immunohistochemistry in distinguishing primary from secondary adenocarcinoma\nF. Integration of negative endoscopic evaluation and immunohistochemical profile to confirm primary bladder origin\nG. None\nH. None\n",
